        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:TextResponse10",
        "expected": "I study Applied Mathematics & Statistics and Economics at Johns Hopkins, but most of what I know about analytics I learned by building systems that had to work for someone. At Summer Atlantic Capital I build the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection system: event ingestion, temporal sequence modeling over event streams, anomaly scoring, and a learning-to-rank layer that prioritizes alerts. What I keep coming back to there is that a detector producing too many alerts just creates a second problem, so I also built a retrieval-augmented explanation layer so analysts can see why something was flagged. That is the same instinct behind visualization and insight work: the output has to be usable by a human making a decision. On a Gates Foundation-funded Hopkins project I trained XGBoost models on a national survey dataset of roughly 10 million records and used SHAP to identify drivers of multimorbidity, then designed G-formula simulations to estimate counterfactual outcomes. At Intelehealth I built evaluation against a 4,169-case clinical standard using stratified sampling to surface systematic failure modes. I am comfortable owning a piece of a data pipeline end to end and checking whether the model actually holds up.",
